Daniel Hardman (Provenant, Inc.): I wrote a paper called Bitewise and Externalized SEDS, that talks about what I think are…

347
00:38:27.590 --> 00:38:34.399
Daniel Hardman (Provenant, Inc.): You know, rational ways to get said-like behavior for different document types, and what it comes down to is…

348
00:38:34.400 --> 00:38:48.170
Daniel Hardman (Provenant, Inc.): some document types, or… and I'm using document in the loosest sense. If you have a genome, there's a data format for genomes. I don't remember what its format is, but it's… it's not a document. But anyway,

349
00:38:48.350 --> 00:39:03.770
Daniel Hardman (Provenant, Inc.): you know, some data lends itself to post-processing, where without deeply understanding the data format and the intentions of the author, you can poke a value in, and JSON is in that category.

350
00:39:03.970 --> 00:39:09.990
Daniel Hardman (Provenant, Inc.): But, others, like, let's say a docx file, a Word document.

351
00:39:10.270 --> 00:39:20.680
Daniel Hardman (Provenant, Inc.): that's a zip file. You can't crack a zip file and insert a SED without changing the actual stream of bytes, in the file.

352
00:39:21.260 --> 00:39:25.630
Daniel Hardman (Provenant, Inc.): Like, you can't do this… the placeholder trick that we do with JSON.

353
00:39:25.630 --> 00:39:26.250
Phil Feairheller: Right.

354
00:39:26.430 --> 00:39:34.790
Daniel Hardman (Provenant, Inc.): So, anyway, different categories of data deserve a different strategy, is the headline, I guess.

355
00:39:35.860 --> 00:39:46.589
Samuel Smith: And so I wanted to… I wanted to add to that a thought that I had, as we're thinking about this. The original motivation for SEDS

356
00:39:46.750 --> 00:39:54.760
Samuel Smith: Was that we're using them as the unique content at Content Addressable Identifier.

357
00:39:54.970 --> 00:40:09.389
Samuel Smith: for… for all sorts of data. But the data was all setifiable that we… that we were dealing with, and it solved a big problem that we had in the early days of Carrie Pie, and that is, because of cryptographic agility.

358
00:40:09.570 --> 00:40:27.350
Samuel Smith: anybody could use whatever digest algorithm they chose to store something in a database as the content addressable identifier. But if somebody sent you some data, you didn't know if it was data you already had, because they could use a different digest.

359
00:40:27.350 --> 00:40:30.610
Samuel Smith: And hence a different,

360
00:40:30.770 --> 00:40:36.200
Samuel Smith: cryptographic identifier, so you would have to re-digest

361
00:40:36.200 --> 00:40:56.450
Samuel Smith: everything according to the digest that you were using for your content address in your database in order to tell whether or not you already had the data. And so we had a lot of code that was basically re-digesting everything to get them so that you could tell if they were the same… if it was the same thing or not.

362
00:40:56.450 --> 00:41:03.999
Samuel Smith: Because… because Cryptographic Jilly breaks the… breaks the… that there's one identifier, because you just change the algorithm.

363
00:41:04.440 --> 00:41:12.019
Samuel Smith: you create a different hash, it's a different identifier. So by using the SED, what we said is whoever sourced the data gets to pick

364
00:41:12.350 --> 00:41:18.499
Samuel Smith: the digest algorithm, and as long as it's, you know, cryptographic strength, which

365
00:41:18.670 --> 00:41:23.370
Samuel Smith: which Caesar only supports cryptographic strength digest algorithms, then…

366
00:41:23.480 --> 00:41:29.219
Samuel Smith: Then everybody could just use that, and you didn't have this duplication of effort.

367
00:41:29.610 --> 00:41:42.739
Samuel Smith: So… so… so as soon as you externalize the digest, if you are also allowing cryptographic agility, then you reintroduce that problem. So one way to solve that

368
00:41:42.740 --> 00:41:54.189
Samuel Smith: or things that can't have SEDs, because just like you said, you can't do that, is that we decide to use a convention where we have a given wrapper that says the wrapper

369
00:41:54.520 --> 00:42:12.899
Samuel Smith: has fields in it that specify the digest that is externalized for the data that can't be setified, but the wrapper itself is setified, and therefore the… or the source of the wrapper gets to pick the digest algorithm, so you use the digest algorithm that's in the wrapper

370
00:42:13.010 --> 00:42:21.019
Samuel Smith: In the field that's designated as the externalized point, and then everybody can then just manage it that way.

371
00:42:21.180 --> 00:42:24.229
Samuel Smith: Did that… did any of the… did that make sense?

372
00:42:25.610 --> 00:42:26.510
Daniel Hardman (Provenant, Inc.): Yeah.

373
00:42:26.750 --> 00:42:30.970
Daniel Hardman (Provenant, Inc.): I think we need a convention like this, actually. This would be a useful thing to…

374
00:42:31.500 --> 00:42:34.139
Daniel Hardman (Provenant, Inc.): Describe, in the community.

375
00:42:34.520 --> 00:42:43.790
Phil Feairheller: So then you have a… so then you have, like, basically a little… like, a little metadata file associated with content that can't be setified that is the certification of that content.

376
00:42:44.130 --> 00:42:46.199
Samuel Smith: Which, which could be an ACDC.

377
00:42:46.330 --> 00:42:46.960
