404
00:46:04.420 --> 00:46:05.170
Phil Feairheller: Okay.

405
00:46:05.170 --> 00:46:07.240
Samuel Smith: So, historically,

406
00:46:07.560 --> 00:46:26.319
Samuel Smith: people don't… a lot of people don't know this. JSON, because JSON's based on a hash table, or a hash mapper, an associated array, originally implementation associated arrays did not preserve order, insertion order, and so canonicalization typically meant alphabetizing the field labels

407
00:46:26.450 --> 00:46:39.089
Samuel Smith: And then… and then… and then canonicalizing that way. So you're always transforming data. You get… you get it, and then you'd have to do… and if you changed or amended or did something like that, there wasn't a natural…

408
00:46:39.530 --> 00:46:46.170
Samuel Smith: preservation of anything, because it could re… re-alphabetize everything. But as of…

409
00:46:46.450 --> 00:46:53.600
Samuel Smith: 2011, ECMAScript, started, preserving insertion order.

410
00:46:53.810 --> 00:47:04.270
Samuel Smith: And so you could depend on insertion order for JSON for non-arrays of JSON, But,

411
00:47:04.370 --> 00:47:24.179
Samuel Smith: Right, because arrays use numbers, and so they don't… they have a different ordering. But if you're just using alphanumeric field labels as a field map, which almost all of these applications use it that way, then it would preserve order. So in carry land, we always just did insertion order.

412
00:47:24.180 --> 00:47:32.489
Samuel Smith: Because then… If you add additional fields, they're gonna be added in the same way for everybody.

413
00:47:33.250 --> 00:47:34.410
Samuel Smith: That's correct.

414
00:47:34.410 --> 00:47:38.920
Phil Feairheller: If you're living with an existing spec that doesn't demand that, then you're…

415
00:47:38.920 --> 00:47:39.590
Samuel Smith: Yeah, so…

416
00:47:39.590 --> 00:47:39.930
Phil Feairheller: those.

417
00:47:39.930 --> 00:47:51.250
Samuel Smith: Yeah, you either have to impose an additional constraint that says, we're going to use insertion order from the very start and round trip it that way, which…

418
00:47:51.250 --> 00:48:04.770
Samuel Smith: Or you have to adopt one of the canonicalization schemes, which is typically some form of alphabization. And I know JSON-LD has its own canonicalization scheme. I guess there's some other JSON ones, I'm not as familiar with them.

419
00:48:04.770 --> 00:48:11.900
Samuel Smith: Sounds like… you're familiar with at least one, Carly. Does FIRE… FIRE should have one, right? I mean…

