00:48:11.900 --> 00:48:14.750
Phil Feairheller: I don't know if it does or not, I'm just afraid it doesn't.

421
00:48:14.750 --> 00:48:15.929
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): I don't think it does.

422
00:48:16.220 --> 00:48:22.840
Samuel Smith: See, if it doesn't, then that's a fundamental defect. You can't provenance information if you can't canonicalize it.

423
00:48:23.100 --> 00:48:23.600
Phil Feairheller: Because.

424
00:48:23.600 --> 00:48:28.710
Samuel Smith: How do you… how do you, in general, right, so this is the problem, this is the problem…

425
00:48:28.710 --> 00:48:30.470
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): healthcare.

426
00:48:30.470 --> 00:48:31.850
Phil Feairheller: No, but.

427
00:48:31.850 --> 00:48:35.210
Samuel Smith: But understand, this is the problem that knowledge graphs…

428
00:48:35.310 --> 00:48:55.569
Samuel Smith: and Jason LD and RDF are trying to solve. They're trying to say, how do I represent the semantics of a dataset, even though the serialization of the dataset is not canonicalizable? Is there a canonical representation of the semantics? And their answer was, you create an RDF graph.

429
00:48:55.570 --> 00:48:59.700
Samuel Smith: The problem is, is that because of hidden nodes, even RDF graphs

430
00:48:59.700 --> 00:49:03.710
Samuel Smith: Are only semantically equivalent to some…

431
00:49:03.880 --> 00:49:16.410
Samuel Smith: some weak, weak, weak, weak integrity proof. So, so, so, so, so you… so there's this huge camera worms that, that is problematic for anybody who cares about

432
00:49:16.580 --> 00:49:29.509
Samuel Smith: security, because cryptography doesn't… doesn't like semantics. Cryptography likes serializations. It can't understand or protect semantics. If the… if the serializations

433
00:49:29.510 --> 00:49:49.379
Samuel Smith: are protected, then the semantics are protected. If the serializations are not protected, then you're trying to protect the semantics with an insecure mechanism, right? Because you can't use cryptography to protect the semantics. So security-first protocol designs start with canonical serializations.

434
00:49:49.580 --> 00:50:02.799
Samuel Smith: And never vary from them, ever, again, because that's the only way that you can ensure that you protected the semantics using cryptography. And that's why that has been the single biggest source of cognitive dissonance

435
00:50:02.870 --> 00:50:12.470
Samuel Smith: whenever I've engaged with the W3C about DIDs and VCs, is this lack of recognition that you can't protect semantics

436
00:50:12.480 --> 00:50:24.039
Samuel Smith: with cryptography, unless you have serializations that are hardened, and that's one of the reasons why we have SEDs, and we setify everything, because then

437
00:50:24.730 --> 00:50:28.359
Samuel Smith: Then we know that the semantics didn't change, because the said didn't change.

